# Fixed 2026-09-29, defect 131's class, lane X4. Symptom: a body in braces
# whose own blocks are indented inside it, braces outside and indentation
# inside, `function main() {` over `if x == 1` over a deeper `print(1)`,
# named each inner body missing, a body that is there, a message per head for
# the one mistake the braces are (lane X1's finding 3); an `else`, a `for`, a
# `while` and a `match`'s arms the same, and a `test`'s body too. Cause:
# inside braces the lexer makes no indent, so the inner bodies reached the
# parser at their head's own level. A body in braces is now read through a
# view of the braces laid out as the lexer lays out an indented block
# (`parse/braced_lines.hero`): the margins inside open and close blocks, a
# level at a time, and the `{`'s report is the habit's one message. The inner
# lines' own mistakes are told; a line that ends where its statement cannot,
# `z = 1 +`, is told where it ends, as with no braces around it, and a
# stray `)` that the lexer took for the braces' close does not open a
# second layout of its own.

function main() {  #~ missing_body
    x = 1
    if x == 1
        print(1)
        y = 3 4  #~ expected_end_of_line
    else
        print(2)
    for i in [1, 2]
        print(i)
    while x < 3
        print(x)
    match x
        1 => print(1)
        _ => print(2)
    z = 1 +  #~ continuation_outside_brackets
        2
    if x == 2 {  #~ missing_body
        if x == 3
            print(3)
    }
}

function stray() {  #~ missing_body
    x = 1
    if x == 1
        y = 3 )  #~ expected_end_of_line
    print(x)
}

test "adds" {  #~ missing_body
    n = 1
    if n == 1
        assert n + 1 == 2
}

# Read again 2026-10-01, lane recovery-b5 (ruling 1, `selfhost/brace_layout.hero`):
# the lexer lays out a body's braces as the block their fix leaves, so `z = 1
# +` over a deeper `2` is the break the lexer refuses with no braces around
# it, `continuation_outside_brackets` where its line ends, in the place of
# the parser's operand named missing. Its join is not offered over the
# annotation's comment, and its parentheses are a guess, so `check --apply`
# writes nothing here.
